### 3.2. Constructing Ego Networks

We’re interested in the ego network of each individual in the above identified user groups and how it changes over time. The notation and definitions are formally introduced as the follows.

Given an observation week tt, a communication medium (meeting, email, or instant messaging), for each individual kk, we define the undirected ego network as 𝒢k(t)={𝒱k(t),ℰk(t),𝒲k(t)}\mathcal{G}^{(t)}\_{k}=\{\mathcal{V}^{(t)}\_{k},\mathcal{E}^{(t)}\_{k},\mathcal{W}^{(t)}\_{k}\}. Here 𝒱k(t)={k}​⋃𝒩k(t)\mathcal{V}^{(t)}\_{k}=\{k\}\bigcup\mathcal{N}^{(t)}\_{k} represents node kk as well as its immediate neighbors33 3 Note 𝒩k(t)\mathcal{N}^{(t)}\_{k} includes all Microsoft full-time employees who have connected with individual ii in the given week tt. The neighboring employees here are not restricted in the user groups identified in [Section 3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.03441v1#S3.SS1 "3.1. Data Description ‣ 3. Data and Methods ‣ Large-Scale Analysis of New Employee Network Dynamics"). (𝒩k(t)\mathcal{N}^{(t)}\_{k}). ℰk(t)\mathcal{E}^{(t)}\_{k} is an undirected edge set which denotes the observed reciprocal connections among nodes in 𝒱k(t)\mathcal{V}^{(t)}\_{k} within the given time window tt. 𝒲k(t)\mathcal{W}^{(t)}\_{k} represents the corresponding edge weights.

