We first focus on each individual’s one-on-one networks where ℰk(t)\mathcal{E}^{(t)}\_{k} is restricted on the communication interactions with only two participants. Following previous studies ([Kossinets and Watts, 2009](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.03441v1#bib.bib37); [De Choudhury et al., 2010](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.03441v1#bib.bib21)), we define the edge weight wi​j(t)=wi→j(t)×wj→i(t)w^{(t)}\_{ij}=\sqrt{w^{(t)}\_{i\rightarrow j}\times w^{(t)}\_{j\rightarrow i}} between two individuals ii and jj as the geometric mean of the number of directed interactions during the observation time period tt. For example, for each employee’s email network, wi→j(t)w^{(t)}\_{i\rightarrow j} denotes the number of emails that ii sent to jj in week tt. To ensure the reciprocity of the observed connections, we define the ego network 𝒢k(t)\mathcal{G}^{(t)}\_{k} comprising the edges with only non-zero weights, i.e., wi​j(t)>0w^{(t)}\_{ij}>0. We consider an alternative group interaction network for robustness test, which accounts for employees’ group communications with less than 10 participants.44 4 Edge weight (wi→j(t)w^{(t)}\_{i\rightarrow j}) in the group interaction network is relaxed as the number of directed interactions, normalized by the number of recipients within these interactions.

